      "latency_ms": 8120.3,
      "prompt_tokens": 25,
      "completion_tokens": 1068,
      "output": "\n\nWater is a transparent, tasteless, and odorless liquid that is essential for all known forms of life. It is composed of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom, forming the chemical formula **H₂O**. Here’s a breakdown of its key characteristics and importance:\n\n### **1. Chemical Structure**  \n- **Molecular Composition**: Each water molecule consists of one oxygen atom bonded to two hydrogen atoms.  \n- **Polarity**: The molecule is polar, meaning it has a slight positive charge on the hydrogen side and a negative charge on the oxygen side. This polarity allows water to dissolve many substances, earning it the title of \"universal solvent.\"\n\n### **2. Physical Properties**  \n- **States of Matter**: Water exists as a **liquid** at room temperature, but it can also freeze into **solid ice** (when below 0°C/32°F) or evaporate into **water vapor** (gas) when heated.  \n- **Unique Behavior**: Unlike most substances, water expands when it freezes, making ice less dense than liquid water. This is why ice floats, which is critical for aquatic life in cold climates.\n\n### **3. Key Characteristics**  \n- **High Specific Heat**: Water absorbs and releases heat slowly, helping regulate temperature in organisms and the environment.  \n- **Cohesion & Adhesion**: Water molecules stick to each other (cohesion) and to other surfaces (adhesion), enabling processes like capillary action in plants.  \n- **Surface Tension**: The strong hydrogen bonds create a \"skin\" on water’s surface, allowing small insects to walk on it.\n\n### **4. Importance for Life**  \n- **Biological Role**: Water is a medium for biochemical reactions, transports nutrients and waste in organisms, and helps maintain body temperature.  \n- **Environmental Role**: It shapes Earth’s climate, supports ecosystems, and is central to the **water cycle** (evaporation, condensation, precipitation).  \n\n### **5. Abundance**  \n- Water covers about **71% of Earth’s surface** (mostly in oceans) and is found in glaciers, rivers, lakes, and even within living organisms.  \n\nIn short, water is a remarkable substance with properties that make it indispensable for life, nature, and human activities. Its simplicity in structure belies its complexity in behavior and impact on the world. 🌊💧"
